# Blog Post Rules

## Title Duplication

The `_layouts/single.html` template renders the page title as an `<h1>` heading automatically.

**Do NOT** include an `#` heading (H1) at the start of a post body that duplicates the `title:` field from front matter. This would produce two identical `<h1>` elements on the rendered page.

✅ Correct — post body starts with paragraph text or an `##` H2 heading:
```markdown
---
layout: single
title: "Some Great Title"
---

Some introductory paragraph...
```

❌ Wrong — post body starts with a duplicate H1:
```markdown
---
layout: single
title: "Some Great Title"
---

# Some Great Title
```

If you need a leading heading-level title within the body, use `##` (H2) instead of `#` (H1). The only H1 on the page should come from the layout.

## Front Matter

Every post must include the following front matter fields:

| Field        | Required | Format / Notes                                        |
|--------------|----------|-------------------------------------------------------|
| `layout`     | ✅       | Always `single` (shows sidebar and constrained content) |
| `title`      | ✅       | Double-quoted string                                  |
| `date`       | ✅       | `YYYY-MM-DD`                                          |
| `categories` | ✅       | YAML list format. Broad topic buckets.                |
| `tags`       | ✅       | YAML list format. More specific than categories.      |
| `toc`        | ✅       | Always `true` (technical posts benefit from a ToC)    |

Example:
```yaml
---
layout: single
title: "Understanding FUSE: How SeaweedFS Becomes a POSIX Distributed Filesystem"
date: 2026-05-27
categories: [storage, distributed-systems, seaweedfs, fuse]
tags: [fuse, seaweedfs, filesystem, distributed-storage, golang]
toc: true
---
```

Additional fields (`author_profile`, `read_time`, `comments`, `share`, `related`) are set automatically by `_config.yml` defaults and should NOT be duplicated in individual post front matter.

## Filename Convention

Post filenames follow the format:
```
YYYY-MM-DD-slug-with-kebab-case.md
```

- The `date` field in front matter must match the date prefix in the filename.
- The slug determines the URL path via the `permalink` setting in `_config.yml` (`/blog/:year/:title/`).

✅ Correct:
```
2026-05-27-understanding-fuse-seaweedfs-posix-distributed-fs.md
```

## Code Blocks

- Use fenced code blocks with explicit language specifiers.
- Common languages used in this repo: `bash`, `text`, `go`, `c`, `python`, `yaml`, `json`, `diff`, `console`, `mermaid`.

```markdown
```bash
weed mount \
    -dir=/mnt/sw \
    -filer=localhost:8888
```
```

## Content Structure

- Start the body with an introductory paragraph (not a heading).
- Use `##` H2 for major sections, `###` H3 for subsections.
- Use bold (`**text**`) to highlight key concepts on first mention.
- Use horizontal rules (`---`) sparingly for major section transitions.
- Keep paragraphs focused and concise. This is a technical blog — prefer clarity over verbosity.
- When referencing a paper or external work, include the full citation (authors, venue, year) early in the post.

## Image and Asset Paths

- Place diagrams in `assets/drawio/` (source `.drawio` files).
- Place rendered images in `assets/img/<topic>/` (subdirectory per topic area).
- Reference images in posts using relative paths from the repo root:
  ```markdown
  ![Alt text]({{ site.url }}/assets/img/topic-name/filename.png)
  ```
- Use SVG where possible for diagrams; PNG for screenshots.
- Name image files descriptively with kebab-case, e.g. `seaweedfs-filer-architecture.png`.

## README Update

After publishing a new post, update the "Published Posts" table in `README.md` with:
- Date
- Post title (linked to the published URL)
- Relevant topic tags

The README serves as the index / table of contents for the blog.

## General Style

- The target audience is engineers familiar with systems / backend concepts.
- Favor depth over breadth: explore a single concept thoroughly per post.
- Use concrete examples (code snippets, CLI commands, config files) to illustrate concepts.
- Tag posts with both broad categories and specific tags for discoverability.